#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069770} :activity/id #uuid "94c7fd02-3d1a-53d9-a362-f4b944c65591" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334 :topic/id #uuid "40c0038d-ae3b-525b-803b-462840adecbb" :topic/math-academy-id 425} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069996 :tutorial/content "Given a quadratic equation in the form \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0,\n$$\n\nwe can find its roots using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this formula, the expression under the square root is called the **discriminant**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = {\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}\n$$\n\nThe value of the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ tells us a lot about the solutions of our quadratic equation.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}>0,$ then we get $2$ distinct real solutions: one with the positive square root and one with the negative square root.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}=0,$ then we get $1$ real solution because the square root vanishes. We sometimes say that the equation has two equal solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}<0,$ then we get no real solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples:\n\n- If $x^2+3x+2=0,$ then we have a positive discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=3^2-4(1)(2) = 1$ and $2$ distinct real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2-4(1)(2)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{1} }{2} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm 1}{2} = -2,-1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+2x+1=0,$ then we have a zero discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=2^2-4(1)(1) = 0$ and $1$ distinct real solution:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{0} }{2} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 0}{2} = -1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+x+1=0,$ then we have a negative discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=1^2-4(1)(1) = -3$ and no real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3} }{2}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79f4f718-3e60-5bd3-8e3b-25b092e91c76" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6630 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b4cec28d-0479-591e-8c9b-bc2853d4190f" :step/math-academy-id 40122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068433 :question/id #uuid "360239d4-c271-52f3-8d25-ed47e7dd52f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10610"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87636457-bdd5-53dd-9137-c070a4e12e84" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532678}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "14778f39-1f57-525b-ae31-35dcbc061af2" :step/math-academy-id 40125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068434 :question/id #uuid "f667bd43-407c-55a9-9821-67a0c1b11a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-10611"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "926a6ba8-ec05-568a-9c13-c9f4d096e89f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532667}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Discriminant to Determine the Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "975d250d-99af-536a-8f81-90af7bf4ecad" :step/math-academy-id 40128 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068834 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068435 :question/id #uuid "5d4530b7-c6df-583f-b98f-8a8f93203295" :question/math-academy-id "e-10612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "452d826b-4cb3-56e1-989a-b025b5d5c955" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532625}] :knowledge-point/title "Choosing a Parameter to Force a Desired Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "6a7f97a5-c2ac-50f8-9a3f-b4d8062db921" :step/math-academy-id 40131 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069774}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069997 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the quadratic equation \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the coefficients $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are all *rational numbers,* and\n\n- the discriminant is non-negative, i.e., $\\mathcal D \\geq 0.$\n\nThen, we have the following possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is a *perfect square*, then the equation has two *rational* solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is *not* a perfect square, then the equation has two *irrational* solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples.\n\nConsider the equation $x^2-x-6=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-6) \\\\  & =1+24 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a perfect square. Consequently, the corresponding solutions are both rational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{5^2}}{2(1)} = 3, -2\n$$\n\nNow, consider the equation $x^2-x-5=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-5) \\\\  & =1+20 \\\\  & =21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a perfect square. Consequently, the solutions are both irrational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{21}}{2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fd92ab0-4173-58f8-8b37-79f116479768" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6633 :tutorial/title "Rational Solutions of Quadratic Equations"} :step/id #uuid "2f5a9238-3e51-5658-bd57-7ad3172c3e89" :step/math-academy-id 40147 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068835 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068436 :question/id #uuid "b92b9a39-e29a-532f-ba2c-f935773d0bf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "adc75098-5640-5e81-a0bc-003fa20c090c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050358}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532709}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadratic Equations With Rational Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "9c9562e4-85a3-56b8-b173-0a8cfad0356a" :step/math-academy-id 40148}] :activity/title "The Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068958}]}
